WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:12.890
We recently did a frequently asked questions
video where we mentioned that James, our master

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:18.289
of microscopes, was on the lookout for a super
rare ciliate with retractable tentacles called

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:20.320
Thysanomorpha.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.270
This video is not a surprise announcement
that we’ve found one.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.079
The Thysanomorpha remains elusive…for now.

00:00:28.079 --> 00:00:35.170
But it turns out that thysanomorpha is not
the only super rare ciliate with retractable

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.680
tentacles.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.160
What you’re looking at right now might not
immediately stand out.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:47.500
You’ve got a sea of green organisms, and
then in the middle is a clear-ish oval.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.010
And that oval is what we’re going to talk
about today.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:56.470
Because over the course of three years, James’
many, many excursions into different watery

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.960
sources have turned up only three of them.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.200
And as rare as the species is, we think the
footage he’s taken might be even harder

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.070
to come by.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.830
The organism is called actinobolina, and it
showed up in a paper that a ciliate scientist

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:23.000
sent to James titled “Protozoa of Especial
Interest from Van Cortlandt Park, New York”

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.570
written by Gary N. Calkins.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.360
The paper was an old one, published in 1901.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.610
And if you haven’t been able to tell yet,
we love old papers like this.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:39.780
They’re often written in a way that feels
a lot more casual than the way academic papers

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.090
are structured today.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:46.750
It can feel like you’re reading somebody’s
journal entry or a travel blog that just happens

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.500
to contain important biological insight.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.050
Calkins’ paper even starts with an extended
description of Van Cortlandt Park, complete

00:01:55.050 --> 00:02:01.650
with railway directions and instructions on
how to reach the exact pond he sampled from.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:07.979
So James read this delightful paper, which
includes an extended passage of the actinobolina

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:09.690
in action.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.299
And the next morning, he found one!

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:18.740
We mentioned that he’s found only three
actinobolina in three years.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:24.540
This is the third one, found the morning after
reading Calkins’ paper.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.220
When James observed his first actinobolina,
he didn’t yet know how rare they were.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:35.700
And his second actinobolina died quickly,
likely due to some kind of temperature shock.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.620
So with this third member of the species,
James made sure to take extra care to keep

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:47.209
it alive and follow it around so he could
observe as much as possible.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:53.130
Now, what makes the actinobolina so notable
are the tentacles lining its body.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:57.440
They can be a little difficult to make out
from far away, but you can see the faint lines

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.890
of them surrounding the body.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:05.430
With great magnification though, you can start
to see the bundles of cilia and tentacles

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:06.819
a bit better.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:12.970
The tentacles can reach to around 2.5 times
the length of the organism’s diameter when

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.769
fully outstretched.

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:21.989
But even more cool than how far the tentacles
go out is how far in they go--like when the

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:27.021
actinobolina needs to move around and doesn’t
want some cumbersome extensions getting in

00:03:27.021 --> 00:03:28.021
the way.

00:03:28.021 --> 00:03:34.690
And when it’s zipping around the microcosmos,
the actinobolina’s cilia seem to have more

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:40.640
of a vibration to them than the usual wave-like
stroke associated with cilia.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.290
In fact, they kind of resemble flagella more
than cilia.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:49.980
But we don’t know much more about why or
how this movement works.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:57.409
In his description of actinobolina, Calkins
writes, “It is when the animal is quiet

00:03:57.409 --> 00:03:59.379
that it takes in food.

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:07.599
It remains for hours, sometimes, without giving
a sign of feeding, but the tentacles are stretched

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:17.650
out to their greatest length and it appears
merely waiting for something to come."

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:22.720
We don’t know if he meant for it to sound
as ominous as it does, but it certainly feels

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.719
appropriate for a tentacled predator.

00:04:25.719 --> 00:04:30.289
Part of the fun of reading these old papers
is getting to see how well these observations

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:35.729
made by scientists over a century ago match
up to what we can see now.

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:43.770
It’s like exchanging letters with the past,
and our little actinobolina is the courier.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:49.249
And it’s all even more exciting for us in
this case because as far as we can tell, not

00:04:49.249 --> 00:04:54.470
many people have gotten footage of the actinobolina
in the midst of a hunt.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:59.909
But while Calkins may not have had the technology
we have today to capture what he saw, he did

00:04:59.909 --> 00:05:05.810
provide detailed descriptions that line up
nicely, starting with the surprisingly particular

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.479
tastes of the actinobolina.

00:05:09.479 --> 00:05:17.379
Organisms pass and pass, but nothing tempts
the hunter until a specific prey comes along:

00:05:17.379 --> 00:05:21.340
the unsuspecting ciliate called Halteria.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:27.369
The Actinobolina stabs it with a tentacle,
which is loaded with a toxicyst at the end

00:05:27.369 --> 00:05:29.749
to immobilize the prey.

00:05:29.749 --> 00:05:54.500
And then the tentacle draws the food in, to
its inevitable end.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:01.059
The actinobolina digests its meal, but as
Calkin himself said, it is insatiable.

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:07.199
So it retracts its tentacles and leaves, seeking
out a new spot to stretch out and ensnare

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:15.360
its next meal.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.029
And here it is again, catching another Halteria
with its tentacles.

00:06:20.029 --> 00:06:24.659
Using the word “tentacle” makes the actinobolina
sound like an octopus.

00:06:24.659 --> 00:06:30.330
But watching the hunt in action, it looks
more like a porcupine with its quills fully

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.330
extended.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:38.949
But a porcupine that uses its quills to skewer
meals instead of protect itself, and then

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:43.199
it retracts the quills so it can rush on to
the next spot.

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:46.979
We might be testing the limits of our comparisons
right now.

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:47.979
But that’s okay.

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:52.369
According to our calculations, we’ve got
about a year until our next actinobolina.

00:06:52.369 --> 00:07:00.089
So when that rolls around, we’ll be ready
with some new ones.

00:07:00.089 --> 00:07:04.959
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:07:04.959 --> 00:07:05.959
us.

00:07:05.959 --> 00:07:10.479
And thank you as always to all of the people
on the screen right now.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:16.779
These are our patrons on Patreon, and right
now, we’re pushing to get to $5,000 a month

00:07:16.779 --> 00:07:21.439
which is when we’ll be getting a brand new
objective, that’s a lens for the bottom

00:07:21.439 --> 00:07:26.550
of the microscope, for our DIC microscope
If you want to see more from our Master of

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:31.580
Microscopes James Weiss, check out Jam &amp; Germs
